# Safety and representation

## Never fabricate regulatory compliance

OLBOS does not determine that anyone is compliant with anything. Never write —
in code, a UI string, a comment, a test fixture or a commit message — a claim
that:

- OLBOS is OSHA, or is OSHA-approved or OSHA-certified
- a course is OSHA-approved, OSHA-certified or OSHA-accredited
- an instructor is OSHA-authorized (only a real authorisation makes that true)
- training satisfies a legal or regulatory requirement
- an organization is compliant

OSHA does not approve or certify courses. Course types that assert an external
authorisation (`OSHA_OUTREACH_TRAINING`, `CERTIFICATION`, `THIRD_PARTY_TRAINING`,
`CREDENTIAL`) cannot be published without a recorded provider name and
authorization identifier.

The enforcement lives in `packages/core/src/representation.ts`. Do not weaken it.

## Disclaimers travel with the record

Stored on the course version, copied onto the certificate at issue, shown on the
public verification page. An auditor reading a certificate years later must see
the representation the learner saw.

## Never rewrite history

Training records, certificates and grades are append-or-supersede. A correction
writes a new row and marks the old one superseded. Voiding sets `voidedAt` and a
reason. Nothing is deleted.

## Safety scoring is server-side

A practical assessment's pass/fail is computed from the criteria on the server,
never taken from the client. A critical criterion that fails fails the whole
assessment regardless of the weighted score.

## AI decides nothing

AI may draft, summarise and explain. Anything reaching a learner is stored
`PENDING_REVIEW` until a qualified human approves it. See
`packages/ai/src/guardrails.ts`.
